WEBVTT

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:29.920
you could

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:30.540
to join me today.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.620
Today I thought we'd do a nice, beautiful little winter scene

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:36.480
that's sort of warm and has a beautiful little glue in it.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:37.440
I think it'll make you happy.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:39.840
So let's have them start off and run all the colors

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:42.000
across the screen that you need to do this painting.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:43.640
And they'll come across in the same order

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:44.860
as I have them on my palette.

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:46.990
Now I've already covered the canvas

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:48.650
with a thin, even coat of the liquid white.

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.110
It's wet and slick, and it's all ready to go.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:52.450
Let's have some fun.

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:55.060
Start today with a little one-inch brush.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.840
Go into a little bit of cad yellow,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:00:59.620
and the tiniest.

00:01:00.570 --> 00:01:03.410
the tiniest little amount of the bright red.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.300
Tiny little amount, can't say that enough.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:08.710
Just work a little bit into the bristles,

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:09.550
let's go right up here.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.900
Gonna have a sun in this painting,

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.210
so we'll have the sun right about there.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.200
Start off just making a little circle,

00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.870
that's all you gotta do, like so.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.720
Okay, now I'm gonna wash the brush.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.800
Now we wash our brushes in odorless thinner,

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:29.970
and just beat the...

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:30.700
the devil out of it.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.250
Okay, now then, I'm gonna go into titanium white,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.150
and we'll come right down here

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:40.010
and get a little touch of alizarin crimson,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.930
just like so, okay?

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.500
Now let's go right up in here,

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.740
and we'll work this in using little criss-cross strokes,

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:49.540
little X's.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.760
Now if you'll use these little criss-cross strokes,

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.140
rather than draw in a big circle,

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.850
it's much, much easier to blend all these colors together.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:01:59.980
I'd really recommend you use these little...

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.050
So maybe I'll add the least little touch of crimson here

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.050
and make it a little bit brighter

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.490
so you can see it better on the set.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:17.260
Sometimes some of these colors are so soft it's hard to see.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.800
And we'll bring it right on out like that.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.600
Normally when we're doing these paintings for television,

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.400
we paint a little brighter than we would at home.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:29.900
So when you're painting at home,

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.740
You paint any way that makes you happy.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:36.580
Bring it right on down like that.

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.330
Okay, I'm gonna go to a two-inch brush.

00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.610
It's a little bit faster now.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:44.950
Go right into the crimson.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.140
Tiniest little bit, tiniest little bit.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:52.370
And now I'm gonna add just a small amount

00:02:52.370 --> 00:02:54.960
of the dark sienna to the crimson.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.320
Make a very warm brown.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:02:58.380
Okay, let's go back up here.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.010
very lightly, right around here,

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:03.710
still making the little criss-cross strokes.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:10.020
Very soft, quiet, gentle little sky,

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.240
but very effective, very effective.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:16.830
This is the type of painting that people put

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.990
in their living rooms to just make it look warm.

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.780
They're good sellers if you're interested

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.740
in selling paintings.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:26.400
So many people write and tell me

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.060
that they're selling paintings

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:29.880
and they never even believe they could paint.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.140
Boy, that really makes me happy

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:33.400
when people write and tell me that.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:34.640
And it's not the money.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:35.860
The money's not important.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.390
It's what it does to your ego inside here.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:41.180
Because if somebody's willing to pay you

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.720
for something you painted with your hand,

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.360
then it makes you want to go home

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:46.520
and paint 10 more paintings.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:47.700
That's what's important.

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:48.720
It's not the money.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:50.540
Going right into Van Dyke Brown.

00:03:51.650 --> 00:03:52.710
Sometimes I talk too much.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:54.170
I have to hush, get excited.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.320
Van Dyke Brown, still making a little criss-cross.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:01.710
Now this is mixing with the liquid white

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.830
and all of these beautiful little things just happen.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.310
Okay, a little bit more of the brown.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:10.710
And right there, right there.

00:04:16.520 --> 00:04:18.340
That looks just like an egg I cooked this morning.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:22.010
Bad news, I'm not much of a cook.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.040
I'll take, just lay a little tiny bit

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:26.580
of this brownish color down in here.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:29.880
And...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:31.020
without even cleaning the brush,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.940
the least little touch of phthalo blue,

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.280
tiniest little amount, okay?

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.430
And we'll just lay a little bit of that,

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:42.480
just here and there, just here and there,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.410
and you can add a little alizarin crimson.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:47.830
I'm just putting some colors down here,

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.090
don't worry about blending them together

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:50.390
or mixing them at this point.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:53.670
All right, let's lay that brush down,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.810
and I have several of these two-inch brushes going,

00:04:57.480 --> 00:04:59.550
and I'm gonna go right up here, and

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.220
Again, blending, still using the little criss-cross strokes.

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:04.690
And you really wanna blend this out.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:10.190
Just blend it, blend it, blend it, working outward.

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:11.610
You don't wanna bring the dark color

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.620
in toward the center, outward.

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:22.730
You wanna blend this until you can't tell

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:25.330
where one color stops and the next one starts.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.530
Very soft and very gentle.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.320
Then very lightly, just take out the brush strokes.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.120
And if you happen to get a hair on your canvas

00:05:35.120 --> 00:05:36.780
like I did there, just take the corner of the brush

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.700
and pop it off, that's all there is to it.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.060
Now then, now then, let's have some fun.

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.640
We'll put a sun up in here.

00:05:43.890 --> 00:05:46.750
And for that, I'm gonna use my finger.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.260
Let's go right here, take a little white,

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.140
and I'm just gonna rub it right in.

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:53.910
Just make a little circle.

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.740
Sun looks a little better if it's round.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:05:58.480
Them square suns sort of bother you.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:00.960
There you go, see?

00:06:01.140 --> 00:06:03.700
You just push that right in there and make a little circle.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:06.360
Now, if you tried blending that, it would smear.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:09.830
So, take your knife, come right up here and watch.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:12.800
Take your knife and just go, whoop, take it right off.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.480
The value, or the color, remains in the canvas.

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:19.600
Now you can come along here and see?

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:23.880
You can blend that, and that easy, that easy.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:25.600
You have a happy little sun.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:27.990
Right.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:29.870
And that completes.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.440
our sky.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:34.320
Now we can have some fun and begin painting.

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:36.980
Now you have to make a decision here.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:40.390
Okay, I'm gonna use a round brush,

00:06:41.810 --> 00:06:44.870
go into Van Dyke brown, a little dark sienna,

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.290
and I'm just gonna mix them on the brush, just tap it.

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:50.820
See, just tap, there we go, let's go right up here.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.160
Maybe there's a happy little tree that lives right here.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:55.560
And then we'll start at the base of this tree

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:57.460
and work upward, so automatically,

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.600
automatically it's gonna mix with the liquid white.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:03.320
and it's gonna be darker at the bottom than it is on the top.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:06.570
And you have your shadows without even trying.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:10.340
Darker, darker, darker down toward the bottom.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.710
Maybe there's a happy little tree or bush that lives there.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:20.120
Begin thinking about the lay of the land,

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:23.240
or the way the ground flows.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.630
I'm gonna have a little hill here,

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:28.530
so that's what I'm again working on.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:32.500
We're not worried a great deal about detail at this point,

00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:36.940
but all we're interested in is the gentle lay of the land.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:39.900
Oh, there's another one.

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.140
See, just let these little things

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:43.820
just sort of fall out of your brush.

00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:44.820
They live in there.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:49.760
Sometimes you have to tap them out a little bit,

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:50.800
but they live in there.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:54.900
And we'll pull a little bit of that color down,

00:07:56.110 --> 00:07:56.870
just the bottom,

00:07:57.940 --> 00:07:59.400
because that'll end up being our shadows.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:00.620
and the snow.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:03.170
So you can use all these things.

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:05.220
Well, you got this little dark color on the brush,

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:06.490
you can use it.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:11.740
Now then, I'm gonna take a little bit

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:13.780
of the liquid black out here on my palette,

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:18.260
take my liner brush, add a little paint thinner,

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:21.340
and then work that brush in there and turn it.

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.620
Turn it, bring it to a super sharp point.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:26.540
Look at that, very sharp, all right?

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:28.480
Now then, in here.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.070
in here, put just the indication of a few little trunks,

00:08:34.370 --> 00:08:36.170
little stems, whatever you want to call them,

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:41.900
limbs, arms, whatever, okay?

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:45.140
Now, one of the questions I get asked a great deal is,

00:08:45.320 --> 00:08:47.380
do you pull it down or do you go up?

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.460
It doesn't matter, try it both ways,

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:53.300
and most people will find it works better one way

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:54.490
than it does the other.

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.360
Now normally I pull down because it's easier for you

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:58.180
to see it that way.

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.980
When I'm painting at home, a lot of times I'll pull.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:03.180
I don't think there's any rule.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.610
You just sort of practice and find which way works for you.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:10.880
We try to show you as many ways as possible,

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:12.640
and turn you loose on the world.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:18.920
Okay, now then, I'm gonna take the old round brush

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.620
and dip it right into a little touch of the liquid white,

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.120
just a little touch.

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:26.700
I'm gonna add the least, least little touch,

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:29.760
bright red here.

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:35.040
tiniest little bit, tap that brush, tap it, tap it, tap it.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:35.760
Okay, let's go up here.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:39.150
Now then, let's put some beautiful little highlights

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:40.190
up here on these trees.

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:43.270
Just use just the top corner of the brush

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:46.070
and begin tapping, see there?

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:49.050
Think about shapes in here,

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:50.470
don't just throw this on at random.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.670
Leave some of those nice dark areas in here,

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:54.370
see the patterns?

00:09:55.130 --> 00:09:58.240
That's what gives your tree shape and form.

00:09:58.660 --> 00:09:59.980
Now if you have trouble making it,

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.440
stick, add a little bit more of the liquid white to it.

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:05.430
I'm gonna put a little more red in there.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:08.450
I want it to stand out a little, oh there we go,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:09.930
there we go, beautiful.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:11.820
You can see that much better, I think.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:19.510
Just go right along here, make some decisions,

00:10:19.690 --> 00:10:20.950
how many trees, how many bushes.

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:22.950
Oops, there's one right there.

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.440
See, layer them, that creates depth.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:29.850
This is where the little bunny rabbit

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.200
is behind that bush.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.420
And then up here, all winter long,

00:10:34.720 --> 00:10:36.040
the little tree urchin stays.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.180
Now I bet you wonder what a tree urchin is.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.340
That's what I call a little squirrel.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.120
I have a big walnut tree in my yard

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:49.940
and the little squirrels come and they eat the walnuts.

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.140
And we have little power struggles

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:54.280
over who's gonna get the walnuts, me or the squirrel.

00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:55.930
Guess who wins?

00:10:56.110 --> 00:10:59.280
The squirrel's got a nest full of nuts and I have none.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:01.290
Let's go right up here.

00:11:05.480 --> 00:11:07.300
Got one of them little rascals that decided

00:11:07.300 --> 00:11:09.740
he liked my garage better than he did out there in a cold.

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:14.760
He moved into my garage, can't get that little devil out.

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:18.360
So I put some mothballs in his nest.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:19.730
That stinks.

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:26.490
Now, once again, while I have this on my brush,

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:29.890
I'm gonna begin pulling in some basic.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:33.010
and I'm not worried at this point about much of anything

00:11:33.010 --> 00:11:34.190
except the lay of the lamb.

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:37.300
Just pull it in.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:49.490
Right, now then, we can take the old fan brush,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.360
the old fan brush with a little white on it,

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:57.150
and we can begin working on some nice shapes and stuff here.

00:11:58.760 --> 00:11:59.620
This is where...

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:02.590
begin laying in the snow.

00:12:04.100 --> 00:12:07.480
And you can go into a tiny little bit of the phthalo blue

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:09.350
and add shadows.

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:12.480
It doesn't matter, in fact it's desirable

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:13.940
if you pick up a little bit of this brown

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.170
and begin working it in here.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.910
Always, always paying attention to the lay of the land

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:26.200
and think where the light would hit.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:29.960
Snow is very real.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.470
really smooth, it has little bumps

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:35.200
cause there's rocks and stones and all kinds

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.120
of little happy things that are underneath the snow.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:40.160
You may not see them, but they cause bumps in the snow.

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:43.570
Okay, maybe, tell you what, let's go right here

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:48.940
and push in, there's a happy little grassy area

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:50.300
that just runs right down the hill.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.400
There, make all kinds of little things happen here.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.300
I like to do these little winter scenes,

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:04.250
they're a lot of fun, a lot of fun.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.950
And they're easy, you're happy.

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:13.560
Okay, I'll tell you what, I'll tell you what.

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:15.530
Maybe years ago, old Trapper,

00:13:16.380 --> 00:13:19.000
maybe he built him a little house right there.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:22.570
So I'll take some of the Van Dyke Brown,

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:25.570
cut across, and get a little roll of paint on my knife.

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:27.470
There you can see, right out on the edge,

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:29.650
it's most important, it's right out on the edge of the knife.

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:29.970
Let's go.

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:35.660
Okay, maybe the little house lives, does now, right there.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:37.640
There it is, see?

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:39.990
Paint that little back eave first.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:44.140
Tell you what, put a front on him,

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:48.590
front on the other side.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:54.260
See, just sort of, in your mind, think what a house looks like.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:57.200
And he needs a little side over here, too.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:06.090
All right, now, take a little white and a little brown.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:10.540
Pull it out very flat, get us a little roll of paint.

00:14:11.900 --> 00:14:14.260
Let's put the least little touch of highlight on there.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.280
Barely touching, barely touching.

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:21.730
Caress the canvas, okay?

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.810
Now, see, we can see that the light's coming from here,

00:14:25.870 --> 00:14:27.170
so this side's gonna be brighter.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:29.460
This one over here, this one's gonna be almost dark.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:31.540
Almost pure brown.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:33.640
Don't worry about too much color over on that side.

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:37.860
Boy, that guy that lives in there is freezing

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:39.380
because there is no roof on his house.

00:14:39.870 --> 00:14:40.730
Let's give him a roof.

00:14:42.180 --> 00:14:43.220
Let's give him a roof.

00:14:43.380 --> 00:14:44.490
There we go.

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:47.420
Just that easy.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:49.470
Now he's got a half a roof.

00:14:51.270 --> 00:14:53.070
Let's feel sorry for him to give him the other half.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.320
on the other side, just sort of bring all that together.

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:05.270
Isn't that easy?

00:15:05.650 --> 00:15:06.590
We got a little cabin.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:10.390
Need a door, don't want him to get locked in there

00:15:10.390 --> 00:15:11.510
and can't get out all winter.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:13.900
There, you got a little door.

00:15:14.540 --> 00:15:17.310
Take a little white, go around the edges,

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:20.660
that rascal stand out.

00:15:21.460 --> 00:15:22.860
I'm gonna take the small knife now,

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:26.310
the little window right there,

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:27.510
just a little blue and white.

00:15:30.030 --> 00:15:31.850
to give the indication of a happy little window.

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:38.160
Okay, we're on the chimney.

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:41.600
We'll use the small edge of the small knife again.

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:44.340
Little chimney, take a little,

00:15:44.780 --> 00:15:46.280
tiniest little touch of the bright red.

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:47.180
Excuse my arm there.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:50.900
Maybe he don't have his fireplace lit.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:53.010
A little snow laying up here on his chimney.

00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.900
There we go, that easy, I'll tell you what.

00:15:57.730 --> 00:15:59.090
Take a little bit of the liquid black.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:02.800
Maybe there's what remains of an old fence.

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:05.000
And it went right on off.

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:07.530
Here, this is just straight liquid black.

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:09.080
There it goes.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:14.860
There's a few old rails left on it,

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:18.110
off into the distance.

00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:23.570
And then, there it went.

00:16:26.150 --> 00:16:26.910
Wash the brush.

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:29.890
Now take a little bit of the liquid.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:32.860
Oh, look at there, a little snow laying up here on this fence.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:40.940
And a little up here on top, see?

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:42.800
And you can just make all kinds of little things.

00:16:45.980 --> 00:16:50.750
Take the fan brush and grab just a touch of that and blend it in.

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:55.090
Alright, now we can fix up the foot of this cabin.

00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:57.750
Take and work on your perspective, look at there.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:16:59.980
You can just cut it right off wherever you want.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:02.620
Whatever you want it.

00:17:04.820 --> 00:17:07.180
Take a little white, a little touch of blue into it.

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:11.520
Come right along here and there's a big old bunch of snow

00:17:11.520 --> 00:17:12.680
laying right along in there.

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:18.990
Create the illusion.

00:17:19.440 --> 00:17:20.460
He's been walking right here.

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:23.650
I'm just using a little tiny, tiny bit of phthalo blue

00:17:23.650 --> 00:17:24.110
and white.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.880
pinker colors, it's got a little pink in there.

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:33.680
We begin building all this in.

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:39.690
Still paying very close attention to the lay of the land.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.840
It's most important that you always keep in mind

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:45.340
the way you want this land to flow.

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:49.670
Don't, it'll get away from you.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.120
Run right out of your paving.

00:17:52.750 --> 00:17:54.990
Pretty soon you'll have land going in every which direction,

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:57.490
so pay attention to that, that's important.

00:17:57.730 --> 00:17:59.050
It's the little details that make you.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.760
Painting's better, make you happier.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.530
When you get done with your painting,

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:06.680
it's nice to step back and look at it

00:18:06.680 --> 00:18:10.960
and feel good about it, want to feel good about it.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:19.620
But you're down here, because perspective-wise,

00:18:19.800 --> 00:18:21.660
it's getting closer to you.

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:27.760
Right, we've got a one side done here.

00:18:30.220 --> 00:18:30.700
Isn't that fun?

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:39.060
Okay, let's get serious, let's get serious.

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:40.620
Find my round brush here.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:42.600
Let's put something over here on the other side

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:43.980
and have some fun.

00:18:44.140 --> 00:18:46.720
Back into my Dark Sienna, Van Dyke Brown,

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:47.860
tap that little brush in there.

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:50.590
See, you really beat that son of a gun.

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.390
This is where you take out all your frustrations.

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:54.600
This is better than kicking your dog.

00:18:54.800 --> 00:18:57.450
Of course, if he's like mine, you kick him,

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.480
he'll take your leg off.

00:19:00.670 --> 00:19:06.530
There we go, right on up, get up.

00:19:06.690 --> 00:19:08.230
See, once again, we're starting from the bottom

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:11.690
and working upward, so it's darker to bottom

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:12.590
than it is on top.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:15.680
Maybe that comes right up into there.

00:19:19.850 --> 00:19:25.710
I like these very soft earth tones,

00:19:27.750 --> 00:19:29.030
browns and stuff like this.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.640
So striking, so effective.

00:19:35.790 --> 00:19:37.790
Maybe to about right there, right there.

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:39.530
Tell you what, let's do, we got this old brush

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:40.450
going, it's so much fun.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:42.470
Let's go right over on this side.

00:19:43.170 --> 00:19:45.210
And maybe right here, I'm just gonna push upward, see?

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:45.910
Just push.

00:19:46.630 --> 00:19:49.680
Look at all the thousands of little leaves

00:19:49.680 --> 00:19:50.580
and stuff it makes there.

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:52.370
Isn't that easy?

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.320
Looks like a bush one right there, too.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:19:58.750
We'll have a couple of these old rascals.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:02.800
Just drop them in wherever you want them.

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.290
Then you take the old fan brush

00:20:06.290 --> 00:20:07.290
and go right back in here

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:09.870
and begin working some of these out.

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.260
Create all kinds of pretty things.

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:15.960
So you can make the lay of the land

00:20:15.960 --> 00:20:17.000
go anywhere you want it to go.

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:29.040
Makes all kinds of little snow drifts up there.

00:20:31.720 --> 00:20:35.990
At times it's just so pretty it hurts.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:44.520
Hey, and let's change the flow of the land here,

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:45.680
the lay of the land, watch right here.

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:49.780
See, take it this way and bring it up in here.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:51.830
Automatically, grab some of that brown,

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:52.670
pull it down in there.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:56.380
Automatically, you can change the lay of the land.

00:20:57.220 --> 00:20:59.980
So now you can see there's a

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.020
separate entity here, goes back farther.

00:21:05.280 --> 00:21:11.900
Pushes everything back, okay.

00:21:20.850 --> 00:21:24.280
Let's take, I'll use the old knife here.

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:27.270
Let's just put a quick little indication here

00:21:27.270 --> 00:21:28.830
of a little tree trunk in there.

00:21:29.210 --> 00:21:29.890
Doop, doop, doop.

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:33.450
Like that, a few little limbs here and there,

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:39.520
little brown and white, touch a highlight.

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:43.650
You can take just a pointed knife, look at there,

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:46.530
scrape through and make all kinds of little doers in there.

00:21:47.350 --> 00:21:50.930
Little limbs and stems and twigs, it's that easy.

00:21:52.430 --> 00:21:59.380
Okay, that's fine, that's fine, there it is.

00:22:00.160 --> 00:22:01.900
With the round brush, let's have some fun.

00:22:01.980 --> 00:22:02.780
Let's get crazy here.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.880
I'm gonna take a little bright red,

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:07.930
a little yellow ochre, a little Indian yellow,

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:10.450
but a very small amount.

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:11.950
Don't get too wild on this one.

00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:14.980
Very small amount.

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:15.920
Okay, let's go right up here.

00:22:16.660 --> 00:22:20.090
Maybe this tree here got a little bit of color on it still

00:22:20.090 --> 00:22:20.710
from fall.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.310
Just drop in some happy little things.

00:22:25.840 --> 00:22:28.500
Once again, worry about patterns, shapes.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:29.970
Think about limbs and...

00:22:30.090 --> 00:22:32.330
and I'm just using the very top corner of the brush.

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:35.920
Just the very, very top corner.

00:22:38.030 --> 00:22:39.710
That though, see all them little things?

00:22:43.140 --> 00:22:45.040
This creates a little warmth in the painting.

00:22:46.410 --> 00:22:49.110
Sometimes I paint winter scenes that are so cold,

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:51.870
whoo, gosh, you almost have to put on a coat to look at them.

00:22:53.400 --> 00:22:55.400
These are very warm and very soft,

00:22:56.740 --> 00:22:58.300
and they go well in people's homes.

00:23:00.570 --> 00:23:01.970
People like these kind of paintings.

00:23:03.970 --> 00:23:06.150
These are the type of paintings I do for special friends

00:23:06.150 --> 00:23:09.460
and as gifts for Christmas and birthdays and weddings

00:23:09.460 --> 00:23:12.370
and all kinds of things like that.

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.360
You know, that brings up another thing.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:19.700
A lot of people ask us what we do with all these paintings

00:23:19.700 --> 00:23:20.640
that we do on TV.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.530
Most of them are used to go into the books that we do,

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:29.710
but the rest of them we donate.

00:23:30.760 --> 00:23:33.100
We donate them to PBS stations all around the country

00:23:33.100 --> 00:23:34.720
and to charity organizations

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:37.530
for them to make a happy buck with.

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:40.660
So if you would like to have one,

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:42.350
one of the easiest ways to get it

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:44.010
is to call your PBS station and say,

00:23:44.090 --> 00:23:45.550
hey, you know, get one of Bob's paintings

00:23:45.550 --> 00:23:46.470
for your next auction,

00:23:48.180 --> 00:23:49.860
and we'll be glad to donate one to him.

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:52.350
And it helps everybody out.

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:55.050
You get better programming that way in the PBS station.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:57.460
He puts a happy buck in his pocket

00:23:57.460 --> 00:23:59.970
and you get a, you get a pass.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:02.980
So keep that in mind if you want one.

00:24:03.890 --> 00:24:04.850
Let's let your station know.

00:24:06.680 --> 00:24:09.260
Just drop in a few little blue and white grassy areas here.

00:24:13.030 --> 00:24:14.670
Kinds of little things going on in here.

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:17.000
All kinds of little things.

00:24:19.700 --> 00:24:21.800
I tell you what, let's use a knife.

00:24:22.340 --> 00:24:23.360
Maybe that's what remains.

00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.090
There is an old fence right here.

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:27.770
There's a post, put one there.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:29.500
Tell you what, three.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:30.380
looks better.

00:24:30.600 --> 00:24:34.390
A little bit of brown and white for some highlight.

00:24:34.950 --> 00:24:36.660
Doop, doop, there we go.

00:24:37.720 --> 00:24:38.680
Maybe there's still one old,

00:24:38.940 --> 00:24:40.600
one old post that's hanging across there.

00:24:41.440 --> 00:24:43.240
Just like that, that easy.

00:24:45.040 --> 00:24:46.340
Okay, let's have some fun.

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:47.940
Let me get a old fan brush here.

00:24:48.150 --> 00:24:49.670
Go right into the Van Dyke brown,

00:24:50.210 --> 00:24:51.300
a little dark sienna.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:55.970
I think, yep, look at there.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:59.140
Big old tree.

00:25:01.050 --> 00:25:03.210
big ol' tree, I put a big tree in a painting one time

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:07.810
and a lady screamed at me, absolutely screamed at me.

00:25:09.310 --> 00:25:11.150
So if you don't want a big tree in your painting,

00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:13.680
don't put one in there and then scream at me.

00:25:15.660 --> 00:25:17.740
This is your world, so you do whatever you want

00:25:17.740 --> 00:25:18.340
in your painting.

00:25:21.030 --> 00:25:24.760
Take the liner brush, little bit of liquid black,

00:25:25.970 --> 00:25:28.870
and we'll just put the indication of a few ol' limbs

00:25:28.870 --> 00:25:29.430
here and there.

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:33.400
Now, sometimes I add a little paint thinner

00:25:33.400 --> 00:25:36.320
to the liquid black to make it really flow easy.

00:25:37.750 --> 00:25:38.530
Just let it go.

00:25:39.170 --> 00:25:42.030
Just let it really work right off your brush.

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:50.260
See, I have some of these limbs that go up

00:25:50.260 --> 00:25:51.220
and across the tree.

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:54.020
Don't have them all just going out the side.

00:25:55.630 --> 00:25:58.030
See, and you can put all kinds of little tiny things in there

00:25:58.030 --> 00:25:59.390
just by stroking the canvas.

00:26:00.190 --> 00:26:00.690
Can you see that?

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:05.600
Just get sort of crazy and let them go.

00:26:10.090 --> 00:26:14.960
Take the knife, a little bit of white, brown.

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:19.530
Let's put a little touch of highlight on this tree.

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:24.700
Barely touch, let the paint grab.

00:26:25.540 --> 00:26:29.390
This is a thick, thick paint, so it'll grab and hang on.

00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:34.620
Maybe this one comes right down in front.

00:26:35.300 --> 00:26:38.330
Okay, see there?

00:26:42.320 --> 00:26:44.180
All the trees you want in your world.

00:26:45.900 --> 00:26:50.070
Okay, now then, take the old fan brush

00:26:50.070 --> 00:26:52.220
and put a little snow down here on his foots.

00:26:55.250 --> 00:26:57.640
Take care of his old foots, big foots.

00:27:02.110 --> 00:27:05.200
Take the liner brush with a little liquid white

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:10.090
and just lay on a little highlight here and there.

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:11.780
Create the illusion of a little snow.

00:27:12.830 --> 00:27:15.030
And I think with that, we about to have a finished painting.

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:19.920
So let's take a little bit of the bright red liner brush

00:27:19.920 --> 00:27:21.430
and we'll sign this one.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:23.520
The old clock on the wall tells me,

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:27.620
I got to leave you for today, but I'll be back next time.

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:29.930
Hope you have your easel set up, glass of tea.

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:30.720
and you're all ready.

00:27:30.840 --> 00:27:33.170
Until then, happy painting, God bless.
